In the previous step, we updated `createBatchFromCacheAsync` in your `VoucherCommandService` to accept **6 arguments** (we added `LocalDateTime scheduledTime` at the end). However, your Controller is still only passing **5 arguments**.

Here is the exact fix for your `VoucherActionController.java` to bridge the gap and pass the schedule from React to your Service.

### 1. The Controller Fix

Replace your entire `createBatchFromCache` method (around line 48) with this code. It parses the time coming from React and passes it as the 6th argument:

```java
    @PostMapping("/create-batch-from-cache")
    public ResponseEntity<Map<String, Object>> createBatchFromCache(
            @Valid @RequestBody CacheBatchPostingDto payload,
            @RequestHeader("Authorization") String token) throws IOException {

        String userId = jwtUtil.getUserIdFromToken(token);
        Integer userRole = jwtUtil.getUserRoleFromToken(token);
        log.info("CACHE_BATCH_INIT | User: {} | ReqID: {}", userId, payload.getRequestId());

        // 1. Parse the scheduled time sent from the React frontend
        LocalDateTime parsedTime = null;
        if (payload.getScheduledTime() != null && !payload.getScheduledTime().isEmpty()) {
            parsedTime = LocalDateTime.parse(payload.getScheduledTime(), java.time.format.DateTimeFormatter.ofPattern("yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss"));
        }

        // 2. Pass the parsedTime as the 6th argument
        String batchId = commandService.createBatchFromCacheAsync(
                payload.getRequestId(), 
                payload.getCommonBatchRemarks(), 
                userId, 
                userRole, 
                payload.getVid(), 
                parsedTime // <--- ADDED HERE
        );

        return ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.ACCEPTED).body(Map.of(
                "status", "PROCESSING", "message", "Batch creation initiated in background.", "batchId", batchId));
    }

```

